Your Turn: Wrap the Plot in a Function
Turn the chunk above into a reusable function that can plot any sample column, and — using an if/else from Arc 3 — let it switch between a histogram and a boxplot depending on an argument.

Please enable JavaScript to experience the dynamic code cell content on this page.


Check your work

plot_sample &lt;- function(df, sample_col, type = "hist") {
  values &lt;- df[[sample_col]]
  if (type == "hist") {
    hist(values, main = sample_col, xlab = "Raw count")
  } else if (type == "box") {
    boxplot(values, main = sample_col)
  } else {
    stop("type must be 'hist' or 'box'")
  }
}
One function, two ways to call it — plot_sample(rawc, sample_cols[1]) or plot_sample(rawc, sample_cols[1], type = "box") — instead of writing two separate chunks you’d have to keep in sync by hand.

Your Turn: Visualize a Range, Safely
Modify the function below so it only plots when there’s actually data in the requested range — using an if/else from Arc 3 to check first.

Please enable JavaScript to experience the dynamic code cell content on this page.


Check your work

visualize_range &lt;- function(df, sample_col, min_val, max_val) {
  values &lt;- df[[sample_col]]
  in_range &lt;- values[values &gt;= min_val & values &lt;= max_val]

  if (length(in_range) == 0) {
    cat("No values found in that range\n")
  } else {
    hist(in_range, main = paste(sample_col, ":", min_val, "to", max_val),
         xlab = "Raw count")
  }
}
